{"id":"el-arroz","es":"el arroz","en":"the rice","sv":"riset","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"]},
{"id":"la-sal","es":"la sal","en":"the salt","sv":"saltet","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"One letter away from la sala, the living room."},
{"id":"el-azucar","es":"el azúcar","en":"the sugar","sv":"sockret","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"]},
{"id":"el-aceite","es":"el aceite","en":"the oil","sv":"oljan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"]},
{"id":"el-queso","es":"el queso","en":"the cheese","sv":"osten","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"]},
{"id":"el-pescado","es":"el pescado","en":"the fish","sv":"fisken","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"El pescado is the fish on the plate. The one still swimming is el pez."},
{"id":"la-sopa","es":"la sopa","en":"the soup","sv":"soppan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"In a comida corrida, la sopa seca is no soup at all: it is the rice or pasta course that follows it."},
{"id":"la-tortilla","es":"la tortilla","en":"the tortilla","sv":"tortillan (av majs)","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"In Mexico, the corn flatbread you eat with everything. In Spain una tortilla is an omelette."},
{"id":"el-chile","es":"el chile","en":"the chili pepper","sv":"chilin","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"Any chili, from the mild poblano to the fiery habanero. From Nahuatl chilli."},
{"id":"la-salsa","es":"la salsa","en":"the sauce, the salsa","sv":"såsen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"Any sauce at all, not only the spicy one on the table."},
{"id":"el-aguacate","es":"el aguacate","en":"the avocado","sv":"avokadon","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"]},
{"id":"el-elote","es":"el elote","en":"the corn on the cob","sv":"majskolven","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"Mexico, from Nahuatl elotl, a tender cob. Spain says la mazorca."},
{"id":"el-pastel","es":"el pastel","en":"the cake","sv":"tårtan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida","fiesta"],"note":"Mexico's birthday cake. Spain calls that una tarta, and there un pastel is a small pastry."},
{"id":"el-helado","es":"el helado","en":"the ice cream","sv":"glassen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"],"note":"Made with milk or cream. Una nieve is the water-and-fruit kind; a nevería sells both."},
{"id":"la-galleta","es":"la galleta","en":"the cookie","sv":"småkakan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["comida"]},
{"id":"el-plato","es":"el plato","en":"the plate","sv":"tallriken","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"]},
{"id":"el-vaso","es":"el vaso","en":"the glass","sv":"glaset","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"],"note":"The glass you drink from. The material is el vidrio, and a stemmed wine glass is una copa."},
{"id":"la-taza","es":"la taza","en":"the cup","sv":"koppen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"],"note":"For coffee or chocolate. Careful: la taza del baño is the toilet bowl."},
{"id":"la-cuchara","es":"la cuchara","en":"the spoon","sv":"skeden","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"]},
{"id":"el-tenedor","es":"el tenedor","en":"the fork","sv":"gaffeln","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"]},
{"id":"el-cuchillo","es":"el cuchillo","en":"the knife","sv":"kniven","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"]},
{"id":"la-servilleta","es":"la servilleta","en":"the napkin","sv":"servetten","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"]},
{"id":"la-olla","es":"la olla","en":"the pot","sv":"grytan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"]},
{"id":"el-sarten","es":"el sartén","en":"the frying pan","sv":"stekpannan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"],"note":"Mexico says el sartén, Spain la sartén. Both are correct Spanish."},
{"id":"freir","es":"freír","en":"to fry","sv":"att steka","pos":"verb","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina","verbos"]},
{"id":"hervir","es":"hervir","en":"to boil","sv":"att koka","pos":"verb","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina","verbos"]},
{"id":"hornear","es":"hornear","en":"to bake","sv":"att baka","pos":"verb","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina","verbos"],"note":"From el horno, the oven."},
{"id":"picar","es":"picar","en":"to chop","sv":"att hacka","pos":"verb","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina","verbos"],"note":"Picar is also what a chili does to your mouth: ¿pica mucho la salsa?"},
{"id":"calentar","es":"calentar","en":"to heat up","sv":"att värma","pos":"verb","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina","verbos"]},
{"id":"el-tapete","es":"el tapete","en":"the rug","sv":"mattan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["casa"],"note":"Mexico's everyday word for a rug or mat, from the doormat to the one under the sofa. Una alfombra is bigger, or wall-to-wall."},
{"id":"el-patio","es":"el patio","en":"the yard, the courtyard","sv":"gården","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["casa"],"note":"Not the English patio. El patio is the open yard of a house, and the schoolyard at school: salimos al patio."},
{"id":"el-pelo","es":"el pelo","en":"the hair","sv":"håret","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"The everyday word in Mexico. El cabello is the dressier one, the word on a shampoo bottle. El pelo is also an animal's fur."},
{"id":"la-oreja","es":"la oreja","en":"the ear","sv":"örat","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"La oreja is the ear you can see. El oído is the inside of it, and your hearing: tengo buen oído."},
{"id":"la-nariz","es":"la nariz","en":"the nose","sv":"näsan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"The plural is narices: the z turns into a c."},
{"id":"el-diente","es":"el diente","en":"the tooth","sv":"tanden","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo","salud"],"note":"A back tooth has its own word, la muela, and that is the one you name at the dentist: me duele la muela."},
{"id":"el-cuello","es":"el cuello","en":"the neck","sv":"halsen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"Also the collar of a shirt: el cuello de la camisa."},
{"id":"el-dedo","es":"el dedo","en":"the finger","sv":"fingret","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"A toe is un dedo too. Say el dedo del pie when it matters which one you mean."},
{"id":"la-rodilla","es":"la rodilla","en":"the knee","sv":"knät","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"De rodillas means on your knees."},
{"id":"el-hombro","es":"el hombro","en":"the shoulder","sv":"axeln","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"One letter away from el hombre, the man."},
{"id":"la-piel","es":"la piel","en":"the skin","sv":"huden","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"La piel is also leather: unos zapatos de piel."},
{"id":"la-garganta","es":"la garganta","en":"the throat","sv":"strupen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo","salud"],"note":"Where it hurts when you are sick: me duele la garganta. El cuello is the outside of the neck."},
{"id":"el-estomago","es":"el estómago","en":"the stomach","sv":"magen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo","salud"],"note":"Me duele el estómago. The everyday Mexican word for a tummy is la panza."},
{"id":"el-hueso","es":"el hueso","en":"the bone","sv":"benet (i skelettet)","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo","salud"],"note":"Swedish ben is two words at once: la pierna is the leg, el hueso is the bone inside it. Un hueso is also the stone of a fruit."},
{"id":"la-sangre","es":"la sangre","en":"the blood","sv":"blodet","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo","salud"],"note":"Feminine even though it ends in -e, so the adjective is feminine too: la sangre roja."},
{"id":"la-cara","es":"la cara","en":"the face","sv":"ansiktet","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cuerpo"],"note":"Careful: cara is also the feminine of caro. Una camisa cara is an expensive shirt."},
{"id":"el-horno","es":"el horno","en":"the oven","sv":"ugnen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["cocina"],"note":"La estufa is the stove you cook on; el horno is the oven under it. Al horno on a menu means oven-baked, and a microwave is un horno de microondas."},
{"id":"la-mama","es":"la mamá","en":"the mom","sv":"mamman","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"What a Mexican family actually says. La madre is the formal word, and in Mexico it turns up in a lot of rough expressions."},
{"id":"el-papa","es":"el papá","en":"the dad","sv":"pappan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"The accent carries the whole word: el papá is dad, la papa is the potato, el papa is the Pope."},
{"id":"los-papas","es":"los papás","en":"the parents","sv":"föräldrarna","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"Mis papás is how a Mexican says my parents; los padres is the formal one. One plural covers a mother and a father, the same way los abuelos is both grandparents and los hermanos is brothers and sisters together."},
{"id":"el-hijo","es":"el hijo","en":"the son","sv":"sonen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"La hija is the daughter. Los hijos is the children you have."},
{"id":"la-hermana","es":"la hermana","en":"the sister","sv":"systern","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"El hermano is the brother. Los hermanos is brothers and sisters together."},
{"id":"el-abuelo","es":"el abuelo","en":"the grandfather","sv":"morfar, farfar","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"Mother's father or father's father: Swedish names the side, Spanish does not. Los abuelos is both grandparents."},
{"id":"la-abuela","es":"la abuela","en":"the grandmother","sv":"mormor, farmor","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"One word for mormor and farmor both. Abuelita is the warm way to say it, and it is what a Mexican child calls her."},
{"id":"el-tio","es":"el tío","en":"the uncle","sv":"morbror, farbror","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"Any uncle, on either side. In Spain tío also means guy or mate; in Mexico it does not."},
{"id":"la-tia","es":"la tía","en":"the aunt","sv":"moster, faster","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"Mother's sister or father's sister, and also an uncle's wife. Spanish has the one word."},
{"id":"el-primo","es":"el primo","en":"the cousin","sv":"kusinen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"A girl cousin is la prima. Swedish kusin does not choose, Spanish does."},
{"id":"el-bebe","es":"el bebé","en":"the baby","sv":"bebisen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"In Mexico a baby girl is often la bebé."},
{"id":"el-esposo","es":"el esposo","en":"the husband","sv":"maken","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"Mi esposo is the everyday way to say my husband in Mexico. Mi marido works too."},
{"id":"la-esposa","es":"la esposa","en":"the wife","sv":"makan","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"Mi esposa is the everyday way to say my wife. Mexicans also say mi mujer."},
{"id":"el-vecino","es":"el vecino","en":"the neighbor","sv":"grannen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"The woman next door is la vecina."},
{"id":"el-novio","es":"el novio","en":"the boyfriend","sv":"pojkvännen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"At a wedding el novio is the groom."},
{"id":"la-novia","es":"la novia","en":"the girlfriend","sv":"flickvännen","pos":"noun","track":"words","level":2,"themes":["familia"],"note":"At a wedding la novia is the bride."}]